// Title: New Network Trace Capture Started Via Netsh.EXE
// Sigma ID: d3c3861d-c504-4c77-ba55-224ba82d0118
// Status: test | Level: medium
// Author: Kutepov Anton, oscd.community
// Date: 2019-10-24 | Modified: 2026-11-21
// ATT&CK: discovery, credential-access, T1040
// Description: Detects the execution of netsh with the "trace" flag in order to start a network capture
// False positives:
//   - Legitimate administration activity
// References:
//   - https://blogs.msdn.microsoft.com/canberrapfe/2012/03/30/capture-a-network-trace-without-installing-anything-capture-a-network-trace-of-a-reboot/
//   - https://web.archive.org/web/20230328220405/https://klausjochem.me/2016/02/03/netsh-the-cyber-attackers-tool-of-choice/
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/9493cf5c565d7a01b8f064e01a51e1b94057e4bb/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_netsh_packet_capture.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where (FolderPath endswith "\\netsh.exe" or ProcessVersionInfoOriginalFileName =~ "netsh.exe") and (ProcessCommandLine contains "trace" and ProcessCommandLine contains "start")
